|~ `::path`, the kind of a path, and what is declared under it: its
   word and the refusals of a read that misses.
   The decisions it exists for: [D114], [D248]. ~|

:path
  |~~ A path, `/a/0/b`, is a value that names a part of another: a key
      of a map or a place of a vector at each segment, a negative place
      counted from the end. It does nothing by itself; the word that
      takes it reads it. `get /a/b` answers the part and refuses one
      that is not there, so a misspelled field is found where it is
      read; `query /a/b` answers null there; `has /a/b` answers whether
      it is there; `put /a/b 2`, `update /a/b ~(add 1)` and `delete /a/b`
      write it, followed as `get` follows it; and a word that runs code
      takes a path as the code
      that reads it from each element, `map /name`, `sort /age`,
      `filter /active`. A path prints as it is written and stands as
      any value does, `/items/1 | segments` answering `[:items 1]`.

      A key is written as a keyword is, bare, `/name`, quoted,
      `/"first name"`, or with its colon when it holds a slash,
      `/:qlang/error`, one key where `/qlang/error` is two. A segment is
      text, as in JSON Pointer, so `/0` reads the place of a vector and
      the key `"0"` of a map. The value itself is `it`, never a path.
      ::snippet~({:a {:b [5 6]}} | get /a/b/-1) answers `6`.
      ::link~(::any/get | doc) ::link~(::any/query | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/pipeline | doc)
  ~~|

:path/isAValue
  |~~ A path is a value of its own kind. ~~|
  ::law~(/items/0 | type | eq ::path)

:path/printsAsWritten
  |~~ A path prints as it is written. ~~|
  ::law~(/items/0/"first name" | print | eq "/items/0/\"first name\"")

:path/readsQuotedAndPathKeys
  |~~ Quoted keys and keys with a path read in a path. ~~|
  ::law~({:"foo bar" {:qlang/error 1}} | get /"foo bar"/:qlang/error | eq 1)

:path/readsNestedKeys
  |~~ A path of two keys reads nested maps. ~~|
  ::law~({:qlang {:error 2}} | get /qlang/error | eq 2)

:path/readsTheKeyOfAMapByThePlace
  |~~ A place reads the key of a map that spells it. ~~|
  ::law~({:"0" 5} | get /0 | eq 5)

:path/printsAKeyWithASlashQuoted
  |~~ A key with a slash that is no name of a path prints quoted. ~~|
  ::law~({:"a b/c" 1} | paths | first | print | eq "/\"a b/c\"")

:path/construct
  |~~ Reads a path as itself; a vector of keys and places becomes one by
      `toPath`, `[:items 0] | toPath`.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:path/PayloadNotPathError
  |~~ `::path` reads its payload as the path it is; a payload of another
      kind is refused, and a vector of keys and places is read as a path
      by `toPath`.
      ::link~(::vec/toPath | doc)
  ~~|

:path/PayloadNotPathError/refusesAVector
  |~~ A vector payload is refused; `toPath` reads one as a path. ~~|
  ::law~([:a 0] | tag ::path !| [type (get /actualType)] | eq [::path/PayloadNotPathError ::vec])

:path/segments
  |~~ Answers the keys and places of the path, a place as its number and
      a key as its keyword, `/items/0 | segments` answering `[:items 0]`;
      `toPath` builds the path back from them.
      ::link~(::vec/toPath | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:path/segments/answer
  |~~ The keys and places of the path. ~~|
  ::vec

:path/segments/answersKeysAndPlaces
  |~~ A key answers its keyword and a place its number. ~~|
  ::law~(/items/0 | segments | eq [:items 0])

:path/segments/readsBackByToPath
  |~~ The segments build the path back. ~~|
  ::law~(/a/0 | segments | toPath | print | eq "/a/0")

:path/IndexOutOfBoundsError
  |~~ A place reads within a vector or a set, from `/0` to the last,
      `/-1`, a negative place counting from the end; `:index` holds the
      place read and `:length` the length of the subject. The last
      element is `/-1` whatever the length, a place that may lie past
      the end is read by `query`, which answers null there, and `take`
      and `drop` answer the part there is without refusing.
      ::link~(::path | doc) ::link~(::any/query | doc)
  ~~|

:path/IndexOutOfBoundsError/index
  |~~ The place the path read, counted from the end when negative. ~~|
  ::number

:path/IndexOutOfBoundsError/length
  |~~ How many elements the value holds. ~~|
  ::number

:path/IndexOutOfBoundsError/refusesAPlacePastTheEnd
  |~~ A place past the end is refused with the length. ~~|
  ::law~([10 20 30] | get /99 !| get [(type) /index /length] | eq [::path/IndexOutOfBoundsError 99 3])

:path/IndexOutOfBoundsError/refusesAPlaceBeforeTheStart
  |~~ A negative place past the start is refused as it was written. ~~|
  ::law~([10 20 30] | get /-4 !| get [(type) /index /length] | eq [::path/IndexOutOfBoundsError -4 3])

:path/IndexOutOfBoundsError/readsTheLastFromTheEnd
  |~~ `/-1` reads the last element whatever the length. ~~|
  ::law~([10 20 30] | get /-1 | eq 30)

:path/KeyNotInMapError
  |~~ A path names a key the map holds, so a misspelled field is found
      where `get` reads it; `:key` holds the key read, and `:nearest` the
      keys of the map it was likely meant to be, near it in spelling as a
      name is near the names of the scope. `keys` lists the keys there
      are, `{:a 1} | keys`, and a key that may be absent is read by
      `query`, which answers null for it or the default it is given,
      `query /b 0`. A path read from the subject of a later step reads
      what that step was handed: after `get /dependencies |` the subject
      is the dependencies, so two fields of one record are read in one
      step, `get [/dependencies /devDependencies]`.
      ::link~(::path | doc) ::link~(::any/query | doc)
  ~~|

:path/KeyNotInMapError/key
  |~~ The key the path read, which the map does not hold. ~~|
  ::keyword

:path/KeyNotInMapError/refusesAKeyTheMapLacks
  |~~ A key the map lacks is refused. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | get /b !| get [(type) /key] | eq [::path/KeyNotInMapError :b])

:path/KeyNotInMapError/namesThePathItFollowed
  |~~ The refusal holds the path the read followed. ~~|
  ::law~({:a {:b 1}} | get /a/c !| get /path | eq /a/c)

:path/KeyNotInMapError/suggestsTheNearestKey
  |~~ The keys of the map nearest in spelling are suggested. ~~|
  ::law~({:emails [] :age 3} | get /email !| get /nearest | eq [:emails])

:path/KeyNotInMapError/readsTwoFieldsInOneStep
  |~~ Two fields of one record are read in one step. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2} | get [/a /b] | eq [1 2])

:path/SubjectNotProjectableError
  |~~ A path reads a part of a map, a vector or a set, one under a tag
      among them; a number, a string or null has no parts, and `:key`
      holds the key or the place read. A path read from the subject of a
      later step reads what that step was handed: after `get /qty |` the
      subject is the number, so two fields of one record are read in one
      step, `get [/qty /price]`, or combined by a form whose args they
      are, `mulOf /qty /price`. A value that may be null is read by
      `query`, which answers null.
      ::link~(::path | doc) ::link~(::any/mulOf | doc)
  ~~|

:path/SubjectNotProjectableError/key
  |~~ The segment the path read, a key or a place. ~~|
  #[::keyword ::number]

:path/SubjectNotProjectableError/readsTwoFieldsInOneStep
  |~~ Two fields of one record combine in one step by `mulOf`. ~~|
  ::law~({:qty 2 :price 3} | mulOf /qty /price | eq 6)

:path/SubjectNotProjectableError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number has no fields. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | get /foo
    !| get [(type) /key /actualType] | eq [::path/SubjectNotProjectableError :foo ::number])

:path/SubjectNotProjectableError/refusesNull
  |~~ Null has no parts for `get`. ~~|
  ::law~(null | get /k !| get [(type) /actualType] | eq [::path/SubjectNotProjectableError ::null])

:path/IndexNotIntegerError
  |~~ A vector is read by a place, `/0`, and a set by the place of an
      element in its one order; `:key` holds the field read. A field of
      each element is read by `map`, `map /name`, and the elements a field
      picks are found by `filter`, `filter ~(get /n | gt 1)`.
      ::link~(::path | doc) ::link~(::vec/filter | doc)
  ~~|

:path/IndexNotIntegerError/key
  |~~ The segment the path read, which names no place. ~~|
  ::keyword

:path/IndexNotIntegerError/refusesAFieldOfAVector
  |~~ A vector is read by a place, not a field. ~~|
  ::law~([10 20 30] | get /name !| get [(type) /key] | eq [::path/IndexNotIntegerError :name])

:path/IndexNotIntegerError/refusesAFieldOfASet
  |~~ A set is read by a place, not a field. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:a :b :c] | get /name !| get [(type) /key] | eq [::path/IndexNotIntegerError :name])

:path/IndexNotIntegerError/readsAFieldOfEachElement
  |~~ A field of each element is read by `map`. ~~|
  ::law~([{:name "a"} {:name "b"}] | map /name | eq ["a" "b"])
